WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.260
Security-sensitive random values do not all serve the same role.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:13.480
Tokens, session identifiers, reset codes, nonces, salts, keys, and correlation identifiers each have different requirements.

3
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.700
A salt usually needs to avoid repetition.

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.120
A token usually needs to be hard to guess.

5
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.340
A key has stronger confidentiality requirements.

6
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.280
A nonce may have protocol-specific uniqueness rules.

7
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.840
Some values need uniqueness, some need unpredictability, and some need both.

8
00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.900
These properties are related but not identical.

9
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.720
A counter can be unique without being unpredictable.

10
00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.860
A random-looking value can still collide if it is too short or generated from the wrong source.

11
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.240
The requirement should be named before the implementation is chosen.

12
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.720
Generated secrets should be long enough for their purpose and should come from a security-grade source.

13
00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:58.700
The length should be chosen based on the value's role and the system's policy, not on whatever size is convenient to print.

14
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.520
Short values may be easier to handle but may not provide enough safety margin.

15
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:12.280
Sensitive random values should not be logged, exposed in diagnostics, included in support bundles, or passed to child processes that do not need them.

16
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:17.420
If the value protects access, treat it like a secret through its entire lifecycle.

17
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:24.100
Random values may require careful storage, comparison, expiration, rotation, and cleanup.

18
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:33.080
A strong random source does not help if the value is stored broadly, compared unsafely, never expired, or copied into logs.

19
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:40.460
Naming and documentation should make the security purpose visible so reviewers can protect the value consistently.
